[The Cooking Spoon — humour; satirical letter for the Days of Awe.]
(Sent by the enlightened cantor Moshe Tanchum, the prayer-leader, to his fiancée Chava-Zlate)
I am hardly sure that you will start the real "wailings" ["yelot"] only after you receive my letter with my "Ḥatimah tovah" [New Year's greeting]. For you keep reciting a whole "Al Ḥet" at me, asking lama'y [why] with others it goes like a "Lamenatse'ah mizmor"; that is: they take each other and get married, and don't wander about like an apostate at "Kol Nidrei". To tell the truth, I too have long been thinking about it, as they say: if it's to be "lashes" already — then one more stroke! But what can you do, the times today are such that before going to the "Avodah" one must make a "heshbon ha-nefesh" [soul-searching]. I have even leafed back and forth through that very "Maḥzor": maybe so and maybe so — and I am fit for kapores, even if you shout "Ḥai ve-kayam"! In short, it follows for us[?] that the "Shir ha-Yiḥud" is never to be missed, it is forbidden not to, and that is why I write you in the tune of "La-El orekh din". For I don't want to make myself too great a "Ha-Melekh" — no, no more; the same story again: if you want to believe — believe; if not — you won't go quickly[?] to "Unetaneh tokef" anyway. — That for many, many[?] reasons I cannot nowadays go straight to getting married; it is, you understand me, no "Tashlikh", where I shake the crumbs out of my pockets by myself — here every "[?] of my brain" must be in its place... Me-hai ta'ama [for this reason] I think you will spare me always taking out the "Book of Kavanot" and reminding me again and again of the years that are being lost; sadly I know it myself, but why should I be ashamed — go talk to a "beggar-king". Here, it seems, I'm already on the right path, standing firmly on my feet, holding on and on[?] to "Ve-khol ma'aminim she-hu" — and suddenly — trrr!, I tumble over at "Kor'im". Now I come to you with a "Tefillah zakkah": look, Chava-Zlate, for heaven's sake don't write me about the wedding; [?] let it seem to you[?] that you are fasting on Yom Kippur.
In short, I hope and believe that most likely the "Almighty" will still help us, and on our street too the great shofar blast will soon be sounded, and then we shall say "Ha-yom te'amtsenu".
Your faithful and devoted
"For ever and ever shall the Most High King reign"
Moshe Tanchum.
—o—
An Overheard Prayer of a Jewish Woman.
Master of the Universe,
protect me from the sequestrator [tax-distraint officer],
from a sub-tenant,
from a [trade] patent [licence tax],
from the hands of syndics[?] ["sandekes"; perhaps Polish syndyk, bankruptcy trustee],
from the "patriotic doctrines",
from police reports and fines ["kares", Polish kary],
from motor cars and from walking crooked,
and let us say Amen.
Kaprizner[?].
